    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new code is mostly well-shaped: the `OwnerHouseholdDuplicateException` + handler pair cleanly mirrors the existing `OwnerIdentityDuplicateException`/handler, `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` is a small single-purpose step wired correctly into the pipeline between assign and identity-check, `AssignHousehold` collapses nicely to a one-liner, and the doc updates (including the `OwnerAddress` cleanup that drops the now-false household reference) are thorough. Two structural issues, though:\n\n1. **`OwnerHousehold.of(OwnerFieldsDto)` is now dead code.** This refactor removed the old `household.equals(OwnerHousehold.of(existing))` comparisons; the only remaining caller of the `OwnerHousehold` factory is `AssignHousehold` via `of(owner)` (confirmed by grepping all references \u2014 nothing calls the DTO overload). The record is now used purely as a \"compute `householdId` from an `Owner`\" helper, so the `of(OwnerFieldsDto)` factory should be removed as part of this change rather than left behind. (If anything, the record could be slimmed to just what feeds `householdId()`, since its value-equality semantics are also no longer consumed.)\n\n2. **`CheckPossibleDuplicate` now re-implements \"same household\" with a different normalization than the single definition it should defer to.** Post-change, a household is keyed on `(lastName, postcode)` in `OwnerHousehold` (last name whitespace-collapsed + lower-cased, postcode stripped). But `CheckPossibleDuplicate` (lines 43-44) still matches the very same \"same lastName + postcode\" concept inline via `OwnerMatch.equalsIgnoreCase(lastName,\u2026)` + raw `postcode.equals(\u2026)` \u2014 i.e. no whitespace collapsing, no postcode strip. These are now two subtly divergent notions of the same thing. The AGENTS/`OwnerHousehold` doc explicitly claims it is \"the single definition of 'same household',\" yet a maintainer who later tweaks that keying rule (the doc even invites \"including city or normalizing the postcode differently\") would silently not affect the possible-duplicate flag. Consider expressing the possible-duplicate match in terms of the shared household key (same `householdId`, different telephone) so the definition lives in one place.\n\nFinding 1 is the concrete must-fix; finding 2 is a coherence concern worth addressing while this area is being reworked.",
